  <metadata>
    <name>South coast direct: Aimere to Nangaroro</name>
    <desc>South coast direct: Aimere to Nangaroro (alternative): Stay on the remote south coast of Ngada and Nagekeo instead of climbing back to the plateau: drops Mataloko, Ebulobo, Boawae and Mbay. High remoteness, heavy climbing per kilometre, water unknown. 102.3 km, +2150 m. Replaces 196.6 km / +4505 m of the main course.</desc>
    <link href="https://github.com/technicaloutdoor/Flores-Race"><text>Flores Race Planner</text></link>
    <time>2026-09-05T06:27:59Z</time>
  </metadata>
  <wpt lat="-8.843000" lon="120.857000">
    <ele>14.4</ele>
    <name>Aimere</name>
    <desc>km 0.0 | kind: port | resupply: basic | water: unreliable | sleep: homestay -- South-coast port of Ngada Regency, ~1 hour south of Bajawa; ASDP ferry to Waingapu (Sumba, Tue/Fri) and Kupang. Exact pier coordinates not found in sources; placed at a low-elevation coastal point inside Ngada near the d</desc>
    <sym>Anchor</sym>
    <type>anchor/port</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="-8.786389" lon="121.391667">
    <ele>19.0</ele>
    <name>Nangaroro</name>
    <desc>km 102.3 | kind: village | resupply: basic | water: unreliable | sleep: homestay -- South-coast district of Nagekeo Regency bordering Ende; farming and fishing villages (Tonggo etc.), grey-stone beaches; an alternative south-coast crossing from Boawae/Mbay toward Ende.</desc>
    <sym>Residence</sym>
    <type>anchor/village</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="-8.830000" lon="120.851000">
    <ele>46.0</ele>
    <name>Aimere Arak Coast</name>
    <desc>[highlight, position approximate] The south-coast strip around Aimere port, known for small-scale distilling of arak (palm/lontar spirit) alongside its role as the Ngada ferry port to Sumba and Kupang.</desc>
    <sym>Waypoint</sym>
    <type>poi/other</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="-8.816667" lon="121.192333">
    <ele>2105.8</ele>
    <name>Ebulobo</name>
    <desc>[highlight] An active stratovolcano (2,124-2,137 m) above Boawae in southern Nagekeo Regency; first recorded eruption 1830, most recent in the 1980s-2000s.</desc>
    <sym>Summit</sym>
    <type>poi/volcano</type>
  </wpt>
